{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>A foolish woman is clamorous</b> — possessor of clamorous foolishness, i.e., simplicity. That is, everything clamorous, whose meaning is simplicity and degradation of speech, is nothing but simplicity, and the etnaḥta on the word הומיה and the revi‘i on the word פתיות will support this interpretation. And it is possible to interpret: as if it said ואשת פתיות ובל ידעה מה, for the word ואשת serves also for the plural, and there are many like it. And the reason: for a woman of foolishness is clamorous and rebellious, but a woman of simplicity is clamorous but without knowledge, and this is ובל ידעה מה, because she has no knowledge of anything, and it is deficient, like ויעבור עלי מה, or the meaning is 'anything,' like חכמת מה על בלימה. And when she has someone to inform her or she knows on her own, perhaps she will take heed and turn away from the clamoring. And it has already been stated that a כסיל is one for whom there is no hope for his correction, and a פתי is one for whom there is hope for his correction."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:21:47.000283379Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}